# TWO PARTS OF ONE COMPOSED LAYER, DRAWN FROM ONE FILING, WITH WHAT EACH CONTRIBUTES. One row
# per ordered pair of part layers that the same fusion absorbs from the same part filing,
# carrying each side's conversion factor.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS THE `INSIDE ONE FUSION` SELF-JOIN, AND IT IS NOT THE OTHER ONE. Two self-joins of
#    composition/parts.sqlc live in this tree and they answer opposite questions:
#      here                       join on the COMPOSED LAYER -> two parts inside one total
#      eliminations/derived.sqlc  join on the PART           -> one part inside two totals
#    The first is a fusion's internal structure, which is what an `asrt:Elimination` speaks
#    about: its `between` names which of ITS OWN parts overlap. The second is two documents
#    reaching for the same supply, which is a referral to a person. Same operands, different
#    key, and unifying them would be a category error.
#
# ⭐⭐ IT IS ORDERED RATHER THAN UNORDERED, AND THAT IS WHAT ITS CALLER NEEDS. A coupling has a
#    direction, `from` and `to`, so reports/coupling_absorbed_by_a_fusion.sqlc must be able to
#    look up the pair the coupling names in the order the coupling names it. Its sibling
#    eliminations/derived.sqlc takes the row comparison `<` instead, because an overlap has no
#    direction and reporting it twice would be reporting it twice.
#
# ⛔ THE REFLEXIVE PAIR IS KEPT ON PURPOSE. A layer is trivially a sibling of itself, and the
#   caller wants that row: a coupling whose ends are the same layer is unreachable through
#   pm.coupling's own key, so excluding it here would be guarding against something the schema
#   already forbids while silently changing what a future caller sees.
-- composition/parts.sqlc crossed with itself on the composed layer and the part filing.
SELECT x.composition, x.composed_layer,
       x.part_filing,
       x.part_layer AS from_layer,
       y.part_layer AS to_layer,
       x.factor_low  AS from_factor_low,  x.factor_mode AS from_factor_mode,
       y.factor_low  AS to_factor_low,    y.factor_mode AS to_factor_mode
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) x
JOIN (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) y
  ON y.composition    = x.composition
 AND y.composed_layer = x.composed_layer
 AND y.part_filing    = x.part_filing
